{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>For this commandment, etc. which I command you, etc.</b> One needs to understand His saying ״הַיּוֹם״ (this day). Further, His saying ״לֹא נִפְלֵאת וְגוֹ׳ וְלֹא רְחוֹקָה״ (it is not too wondrous, etc., nor is it distant) — but is not remoteness included within wondrousness? Further, His saying ״וְלֹא מֵעֵבֶר לַיָּם״ (and it is not beyond the sea), etc. — this is fine as to His saying ״לֹא בַשָּׁמַיִם הִיא״ (it is not in the heavens), for that is something unattainable to grasp, since who can go up to the heavens? But beyond the sea — surely ships can cross and take it from there, even if it were in the most distant islands of the sea." "<b>Or it may be</b> that the verse intends two specific types of impediment: one from the aspect of grasping knowledge of the Torah itself, and the second from the aspect of fulfilling its commandments. Corresponding to the impediment of knowing the Torah He said ״לֹא נִפְלֵאת הִיא״ (it is not too wondrous), etc., and He went back and explained what wondrousness is, and said ״לֹא בַשָּׁמַיִם״ (it is not in the heavens), etc. — corresponding to the absence of its attainment, He said ״לֹא בַשָּׁמַיִם הִיא״ (it is not in the heavens), etc., as it was at the beginning, for Moses went up to the heavens and took it for us, and behold it is before us." "<b>And corresponding to</b> the impediment of grasping the fulfillment of its commandments, He said ״וְלֹא רְחֹקָה״ (nor is it distant), and He went back and explained what distance is, and said ״וְלֹא מֵעֵבֶר לַיָּם הִיא לֵאמֹר״ (and it is not beyond the sea, to say), etc. — meaning, just as happened to Moses, that even though he went up to heaven and brought down the Torah, the attainment of fulfilling its commandments that depend on the land was withheld from him — and these are many and numerous, almost the majority of the Torah — unlike them, who succeeded in crossing the sea to come to the land to fulfill its commandments." "<b>And although</b> He said ״מִי יַעֲבָר לָנוּ אֶל עֵבֶר הַיָּם וְיִקָּחֶהָ לָּנוּ״ (who will cross for us to the other side of the sea and take it for us), He combined together the impediment of grasping it with the impediment of fulfilling its commandments; but in any case, the principal thing intended by ״מֵעֵבֶר לַיָּם״ (beyond the sea) is the impediment of fulfilling its commandments." "<b>And He was precise</b> to say ״הַיּוֹם״ (this day) — meaning, at the time when they were about to enter the land, for then the matter was close to them to do. And he called the Jordan a sea because it flows into the Salt Sea, as stated in Joshua (Joshua 3:16): ״וְהַיֹּרְדִים עַל יָם הָעֲרָבָה יָם הַמֶּלַח תַּמּוּ נִכְרָתוּ״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:20:34.807644483Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}